:: \ Does adjusting IPD improve cybersickness symptoms?\  {"position":"800,200","size":"100,100"}
# Does adjusting IPD improve cybersickness symptoms?
Stanney et al. (2020) investigated whether poor headset fit could explain differences in cybersickness between men and women.

### In one experiment:
* Participants were grouped according to whether their IPD was adequately accommodated by the headset.
* They also differed in their history of motion sickness (low or high susceptibility).
* Cybersickness was measured immediately after VR exposure and at 15-minute intervals over the following hour using the Simulator Sickness Questionnaire (SSQ).

### What did they find? 
* When properly fitted, women experienced similar levels of cybersickness to men, regardless of motion sickness history.
* Women with poor headset fit and high motion sickness susceptibility experienced more persistent symptoms, taking longer to recover.

<img src=https://github.com/amyw143/Is-your-virtual-reality-headset-sexist/blob/main/Stanney%20et%20al.%20aftereffects%20graph.jpg?raw=true> 
<p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:#9AA3B8;"><b>Figure 2</b><br><i>Group mean SSQ total scores at baseline (BL) and five aftereffect (AE) periods following VR exposure. The results illustrate differences in cybersickness and recovery across IPD-fit and motion sickness history groups. </i><br>Figure from Stanney et al. (2020).</p>

These findings suggest that improving IPD compatibility could reduce disparities in cybersickness between men and women, while improving the reliability of VR research.

[[ Let's take a look at existing headsets ]] 
