:: \ Interpupillary distance\  {"position":"1100,200","size":"100,100"}
# Interpupillary distance (IPD) and lens fit

<img src=https://github.com/amyw143/Is-your-virtual-reality-headset-sexist/blob/main/IPD%20and%20lens%20fit%20-%20main.jpg?raw=true>
IPD is the distance between the centres of the pupils. Headset lenses must align with the user's pupils. When lens spacing differs from IPD, users may experience blur, distortion, eyestrain, and, in some cases, nausea. Adjusting your headset's IPD to your own measurements can prevent these uncomfortable effects. 

### IPD Sex differences 
IPD ranges and mean values differ between sexes, with males tending toward a wider IPD.

<img src=https://github.com/amyw143/Is-your-virtual-reality-headset-sexist/blob/main/IPD%20and%20lens%20fit%20-%20L2.jpg?raw=true>

<p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:#9AA3B8;"><b>Figure 1</b><br><i>Interpupillary distance range and mean in females and males.</i><br>Data from Stanney et al. (2020).</p>

### How do you measure IPD?

<img src=https://github.com/amyw143/Is-your-virtual-reality-headset-sexist/blob/main/IPD%20and%20lens%20fit%20-%20L3.jpg?raw=true
<p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:#9AA3B8;"><b>Example IPD measurement.</b>

You can measure your IPD by standing in front of a mirror with a ruler. Placing the ruler flat against your eyebrows or right under your eyes, align the zero mark with the center of your left pupil. Read the mark that lines up with the center of your right pupil, and repeat the measurements 2 to 3 times to ensure consistency. 

[[ How do you adjust IPD? ]]

[(link-reveal: "Click for Sources")[
<a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7805626/"> Virtual Reality Is Sexist: But It Does Not Have to Be</a>]]


:: \ Let's take a look at existing headsets\  {"position":"600,200","size":"100,100"}
# With these ranges in mind, let's take a look at existing headsets.

''Female IPD Range:'' 51–74.5 mm, mean 61.7 mm

''Male IPD Range:'' 53–77.5 mm, mean 64.0 mm

<img src="https://github.com/amyw143/Is-your-virtual-reality-headset-sexist/blob/main/Let's%20take%20a%20look%20at%20existing%20headsets.jpg?raw=true">

1. *Sony PlayStation VR* — 48–78 mm ✓ Fits full female range
2. *Samsung Gear VR* — Fixed 62 mm ✗ Fits approximately 10% of females
3. *Oculus Rift* — 58–72 mm ✗ Excludes the smallest ~15% of females
4. *Oculus Rift S* — 61.5–65.5 mm ✗ Excludes the smallest ~45% and largest ~15% of females
5. *Oculus Quest* — 56–74 mm ✗ Excludes the smallest ~7% of females
6. *HTC Vive* — 60.5–74.4 mm ✗ Excludes the smallest ~35% of females
7. *HTC Vive Pro* — 60.9–74 mm ✗ Excludes the smallest ~40% of females
8. *Meta Quest 3* — 53–75 mm ✗ Does not accommodate the smallest reported female IPDs

That's interesting... a good proportion of popular headsets do not account for the full female IPD range. Does that mean VR headsets are sexist?

[[ But first, how do we define sexism? ->What is Sexism?]]
