:: Factors {"position":"900,25","size":"100,100"}
# Contributing Factors
Click on the links to learn more about some potential factors influencing the gender differences in cybersickness. 

* (link-reveal: "Gaming experience")[
Repeated exposure to VR can reduce cybersickness in a process known as adaptation. Men tend to participate in VR gaming at a higher rate than women, so experience could be a protective factor (Kelly et al., 2021).
]
* (link-reveal: "Susceptibility to motion sickness")[
In general, women tend to report greater levels of motion sickness than do men. If women tend to be more aware of motion sickness, this might lead to an overestimation of gender differences (Stanney et al., 2020).
]
* (link-reveal: "Female hormonal cycle")[
Motion sickness susceptibility changes throughout the menstrual cycle, and females may be less tolerant of VR exposure compared to males during these peaks (Golding et al., 2005).
]
* (link-reveal: "VR content")[
Not all VR games are made equal. For example, one study found that there was no gender difference when participants played a stationary puzzle game, but a gender difference emerged once participants had to navigate through a virtual environment (Munafo et al., 2017). The nature of the VR activity may play a role in cybersickness.
]

Headset design may also play a role.

[[ Examine a VR headset. ]]  

