:: Flight simulator case study {"position":"825,725","size":"100,100"}
### Flight simulator case study

In a study by Lewkowicz et al. (2020) using a flying simulator called Gyro-IPT, which incorporates elements of mixed and virtual reality, all participating pilots were men. The gender identities of the non-pilot participants were not reported. The researchers found that levels of motion sickness were similar between pilots and non-pilots.

Through a lack of gender reporting and possible participant selection bias, female pilots may fall through the gaps. Given that previous studies have found that women experience more motion sickness than men, the simulator could create an unfair disadvantage during evaluative training. Women who perform poorly due to motion sickness could be judged as less capable pilots, even if their flying skills are comparable.

