:: Institutional Sexism {"position":"200,400","size":"100,100"}
## Institutional Sexism 

Sexism as defined by Murphy (1992) is an institutional structure level is a set of cultural and social standards that result in women having a social standards that result in women have diminished set of equal opportunities and freedom in society.

</style>
<img
src=https://i.imgur.com/D0qcKfG.jpeg
</div>

A study by Islam et al. (2025) found that in technology leadership roles, there is a prizing and rewarding of traits deemed masculine, "such as technical acumen and aggressiveness, while undervaluing collaboration and mentorship. 

When women took on these traits, they were often punished for violating people's ideas of what it means to be a woman (Islam et al., 2025).

Men are seen in institutions as having more potential (Benson et al., 2023) than women, accounting for 30-50% of promotion differences that favour women versus men. 

This potential is seen in industry as well as academia (Moss-Racusin et al., 2012), resulting in fewer opportunities for women to be key decision-makers in the design and development of products (Benson et al., 2023).

