:: Is your virtual reality headset sexist?\  {"position":"500,25","size":"100,100"}
# Is your VR headset sexist?

Cybersickness limits the adoption of VR in training, clinical, and research settings, and several studies report higher susceptibility in women. 

Can hardware that looks neutral still fit some users better than others?

<img src="https://i.imgur.com/K95OsI8.jpeg" alt="Person wearing a VR headset" style="max-width:100%;">

[[Begin-> What is Cybersickness?]]


:: Personal Sexism {"position":"100,600","size":"100,100"}
## Personal Sexism

Manne (2018) describes sexism at a personal level as the belief of women's inferiority, non-importance, and subservience to men 

Comments in workplaces, especially technology and design can cause women to drop out in search of more inclusive workplaces. The lack of diversity in workplaces can lead to design choices that are alienating to and less overall profits.

Over 50% of women are likely to leave the technology industry by the age of 35 with sexism, toxic work cultures and a lack of equal opportunity driving the decision to leave (ISACA, 2026, V. Singh, 2023)

47% of women in tech (report having experienced explicit gender discrimination in the workplace (Kuteesa et al., 2024)

</style>
<img
src=https://i.imgur.com/mMRDviJ.jpeg
</div>

An unwelcoming workplace causes people to leave, taking their unique insights and opinions with them. Without diverse input and open dialogue, product design may suffer. 

