:: Sexism at a Scientific Level {"position":"700,600","size":"100,100"}
## Sexism at a Scientific Level

Sexism at a scientific level either ignores biological differences or reinforces them as a reason to provide more resources and opportunities to men. These biases can also influence how research is conducted and whose experiences are represented.

A 2021 systematic review by MacArthur et al., *You're Making Me Sick*, examined how VR research considers gender and cybersickness. The authors identified several limitations:

- **Limited measurement:** Only 20 of 96 papers attempted to measure cybersickness using different assessment tools.
- **Lack of gender reporting:** Most VR studies did not report or investigate gender effects.
- **Inconsistent study designs:** Differences in experimental tasks, VR content, and technical specifications make findings difficult to compare.
- **Lack of demographic data:** Insufficient reporting of participant characteristics limits the ability to investigate sex and gender differences.
- **Participant selection bias:** Recruitment practices and inclusion/exclusion criteria may systematically underrepresent certain populations.

The authors also highlighted the possibility of underrepresentation of female participants and authors. Interestingly, one meta-analysis (Peck et al., 2020) found smaller increases in simulator sickness in studies with higher proportions of female participants, illustrating the complexity of interpreting gender differences.

Together, these limitations make it difficult to establish whether women experience cybersickness differently from men. When scientific research fails to account for the diversity of its participants, its findings risk reflecting only a subset of the population rather than being representative of everyone. 

