:: Sexism in Design {"position":"650,475","size":"100,100"}
## Sexism in Design

Sexism in the design according to Criado Perez (2019) is the impact of technological products in which unconsciousness and consciousness bias (I methodology) result in products, services and technology less suited to serve women.

The disparity between men and women within virtual reality research results in studies that can reinforce sexist product design

</style>
<img
src=https://i.imgur.com/nendVcg.png
</div>

Figure 5: Each icon is aligned with the gender of 50 participants from human-subject experiments in the Proceedings of the IEEE Virtual Reality Conferences from 2015-2019.

An almost 2 male to 1 female participant ration exists at the conference, potentially skeweing the virtual reality research. With Peck et al (2020) and Kelly et al (2023) finding that as sample size that included women went up findings of motion sickness went up.

