:: Sexism in Video Game Design {"position":"500,600","size":"100,100"}
## Sexism in Video Game Design

Sexism in video games is described by Denia Mondéjar et al. (2026) as the widespread treatment of gaming as a male-dominated environment. It often appears as hypersexualization and the fragility of female characters in game design, alongside verbal harassment and identity policing. Within video game companies, women experience harassment and unfair treatment. 

Detroit: Become Human is an unfortunate example of a great game in which the definition of ''sexism in video games'' is exemplified.

In Detroit: Become Human, highly sexualized depictions of women and scenes of gendered violence have bothered many. The lead director, David Cage, has dismissed these claims of sexism within his games and real-world claims of sexism within his video game studio, Lucid Deams.
 
